This AV amplifier is complicated to set up, and the workmanship is rough, and it generates a lot of heat.
The sound needs to be used for a while before being reviewed!
I just got the DN1080 in German version on August 14. It looks bad in terms of workmanship.
1. The front panel and the knob are made of plastic.
2. The back panel has an audio input jack and the speaker output terminals are not good. A CD amplifier unit with a price of 1000-2000 yuan is much better than this one.
3. The top cover is relatively thin, and the transformer side is sunken in, so I had to open the box and use my hand to restore it. The top cover and the front panel are loose.
4. The center of gravity of the whole machine is biased to the transformer end, and it is awkward to move it.
It feels like a SONY entry-level machine, just like the workmanship, made in Malaysia.
It seems Sony would be better off manufacturing in China than with Xiaoma.
5. The machine is very hot. When watching a movie in a two-channel mode for about 2 hours, the top cover is about 40-50℃ when touched by hand. I don’t know if the transformer is too big and generates a lot of heat.
Now I use a switch-mode power supply, and this stupid transformer probably weighs 4 kg.
Sony should use active silent fans in the machine, and after opening the box, only large aluminum heat sinks are seen on the pipes.
I don't feel too good.
First, the box is not very sturdy and deforms when you press it; second, the design on the back does not prevent the ABS flakes on the box surface from falling off; third, the box is just a piece of cloth wrapped around a rod; and so on. There are many things that are not satisfactory, such as the fact that the rod is exposed; finally, the latch is a bit difficult to use.
PS: The wheels do feel pretty good.
